	affiliations = {Mayo Clinic, Rochester, United States; Department of Communication Studies, Department of Communication Studies, Tuscaloosa, United States},
	abstract = {This study examines the subjective process of catching feelings, which are the unintentional, unexpected, and surprising yet desirable feelings that can arise during relationship development. In a two-step collection process of focus groups (n = 17) and individual interviews (n = 15), collegiate adults completed guided narrative mapping and elicitation interviews. Analysis of the narrative maps and interviews identified three key ways individuals make sense of the catching feeling process: (1) finding the right “moment” to communicate feelings and take action, (2) “picking up the pace” by increasing the frequency and depth of communication, and (3) seeing catching feeling as a “choose your own adventure” experience, where certain decisions lead to certain relational outcomes. These findings offer insight into perceptions and expectations of adult contemporary romantic relationships when they make decisions about relationship initiation in catching feelings experiences. The visual illustrations of their maps draw on their experiences and tie contemporary lexica with actualized or perceived experiences. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
